📜 FTAI Grammar and Syntax v1.6 — Changelog

Release Date: 2025-04-28
Version: 1.6
Status: Finalized

⸻

🚀 Major Improvements
	•	Emphasis Parsing Resilience:
Parsers must treat unmatched **bold** markers as plain text without error.
	•	Structured Tables Enforcement:
Tables must include a header row, an immediate --- divider, and maintain consistent column counts across rows.
No blank header cells allowed to ensure deterministic parsing.
	•	Namespace Expansion:
Reserved prefixes expanded to include @sys*, @internal*, @debug*, and @_* for future system and infrastructure tags.
	•	Hierarchy Hardening:
Maximum depth restricted to two levels (@section, @@subsection).
Deeper nesting requires external file inclusion via @include.
	•	Type-Hint System Introduced:
Tags can now use type-hint suffixes (e.g., :int, :float, :bool, :date) to standardize parsing across implementations.
Missing hint defaults to UTF-8 string.
	•	Clarified Purpose and Core Syntax:
Improved explanations for tag usage, block closure, quoting rules, and human-readable emphasis.
	•	Canonical Mini Example Added:
A complete working .ftai file example is included to demonstrate proper structure, block nesting, table usage, and feedback handling.

⸻

🧠 Guiding Principles Reinforced
	•	Deterministic:
Parsing must always produce the same AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) across platforms and parsers.
	•	Traceable:
Specifications must embed rationale, structure history, and support audit hashing.
	•	Minimal:
No unnecessary punctuation, markup, or verbosity in document construction.
	•	Composable:
Documents must bridge cleanly and losslessly into JSON, YAML, or other structured formats without extra transformation layers.

